---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const rows = [
  { n: 1, vendor: 'Acme Supply Co.', cc: 'CC-4021', amount: '$4,200.00', state: 'ok', tone: 'success', word: 'Ready', msg: 'Ready to import' },
  { n: 2, vendor: 'Globex Industrial', cc: 'CC-1180', amount: '$56,000.00', state: 'warning', tone: 'warning', word: 'Check', msg: 'Matched "Globex" to Globex Industrial' },
  { n: 3, vendor: 'Nobody Ltd', cc: 'CC-1180', amount: '$900.00', state: 'error', tone: 'danger', word: 'Cannot import', msg: 'No vendor matches "Nobody Ltd"' },
  { n: 4, vendor: 'Initech GmbH', cc: 'CC-99', amount: '$100.00', state: 'error', tone: 'danger', word: 'Cannot import', msg: 'Cost centre "CC-99" is not CC-nnnn' },
];

const contract = `POST /import   action=validate   csv=<pasted rows>
  -> 200, the SAME screen with every row classified. Nothing is created.

POST /import   action=apply      csv=<the validated rows>
  -> 200, a success alert with the count APPLIED, and the rows that could
     not be applied still listed. Never a bare "done".

Row classification is the SERVER's, and only two of the three states put a
tint on the row:
  ok      -> no data-row-state. A normal row; its confirmation is the badge.
  warning -> data-row-state="warning"   applied, with a caveat
  error   -> data-row-state="error"     not applied, ever`;
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: staging / batch result">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: whoever maintains data in bulk — an
    uploaded file, an interface run, a mass posting.
    <strong>How often</strong>: month-end and whenever a supplier sends a
    spreadsheet. <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: every submitted
    row either became a record or is still on screen saying why it didn't.
    This is the landing place that makes the
    <a href={base + '/patterns/bulk-actions'}>bulk action</a> contract work
    for data the user has not seen yet. <strong>Not for a handful of
    records</strong> — three or four rows do not need a validate/apply split
    with per-row verdicts; edit them one at a time in a plain
    <a href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>detail form</a>. This pattern
    earns its complexity only once the count is large enough that a human
    cannot eyeball every row before committing.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The state after validation</h2>
    {/* KEEPS role="alert", deliberately. In a real screen this banner is the
        RESULT of POST /import action=validate (see the data contract below),
        so it arrives after load and the role is what a consumer copies. The
        docs can only render the post-action state statically; that is a
        limitation of the page, not of the markup. */}
    <div class="bo-alert bo-alert--warning" role="alert">
      <p><strong>1 ready, 1 to check, 2 cannot import.</strong> Every row
      below says which it is and why.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-3)">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">#</th><th scope="col">Vendor</th>
          <th scope="col">Cost centre</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr data-row-state={r.state === 'ok' ? undefined : r.state}>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">{r.n}</td>
              <td>{r.vendor}</td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{r.cc}</td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">{r.amount}</td>
              <td>
                <span class={`bo-badge bo-badge--${r.tone}`}>{r.word}</span>
                <span class="bo-u-text-muted"> {r.msg}</span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="bo-form-actions">
      <button class="bo-btn" type="button">Apply 2 valid rows</button>
      <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--ghost" href="#">Start over</a>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Zero new components. The row tints are the
    data table's existing <code>data-row-state</code>; the reason on each
    row is a <a href={base + '/components/badge'}>badge</a> plus words, so
    the tint is never the only signal. <strong>There is no "ok" tint</strong>
    — a row with nothing wrong is a normal row, and a third colour would
    make the two that need attention harder to find.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Intake</strong> — a paste box or
      <a href={base + '/components/file-upload'}>file upload</a>. Whatever the
      source, the rows arrive as text the server can parse.</li>
      <li><strong>Validate action</strong> — explicitly separate from apply,
      because the whole point is seeing the outcome before committing to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Summary</strong> — an <a href={base + '/components/alerts'}>alert</a>
      counting all three outcomes in one sentence.</li>
      <li><strong>Staging table</strong> — one row per submitted line, in the
      order submitted, each carrying its own verdict.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply action</strong> — names the number it will act on
      ("Apply 2 valid rows"), and is disabled when that number is zero.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <pre><code>{contract}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Two posts to one endpoint, distinguished by an
    action field — not two endpoints, because validate and apply must agree
    on the parse exactly, and the cheapest way to guarantee that is one code
    path. The validated rows travel back in the second request, so the server
    never holds per-user staging state between them.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Deliberately no 4xx for a bad
    row.</strong> Both actions always return 200 — a row failing validation
    is DATA (<code>data-row-state="error"</code> in the classified result),
    never a transport-level error, which is what lets one response carry a
    mix of ok/warning/error rows in the same screen. A genuinely malformed
    request (unparseable file, missing action field) is the one case that
    IS a 4xx, and gets a plain error banner rather than a partial result.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Nothing pasted yet</td><td>just the intake box — no empty table, no zero-count summary</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validated, all ready</td><td>a success alert; the apply button names the count</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validated, mixed</td><td>counts for all three outcomes, and the two that need attention are tinted</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validated, none applicable</td><td>the apply button is <strong>disabled</strong> — the one place disabled beats absent, because the control's whole job is to tell you the count is zero</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Empty submission</td><td>the "nothing to validate" empty state, not a table with no rows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>After apply</td><td>a success alert with the number APPLIED, and every un-appliable row still listed to fix and re-submit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Re-validated</td><td>verdicts are recomputed from scratch. Like bulk actions, per-row results are TRANSIENT — a stale "cannot import" on a row the user just fixed is worse than no message</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>fully functional — this page has no JS dependency. Every row's tint (<code>data-row-state</code>) and verdict badge are server-rendered classification, not JS state; the "Apply" and "Start over" controls shown here are static illustration (<code>type="button"</code>, no script anywhere on the page), since a real staging screen posts the validate/apply actions from the Data contract above regardless of JS</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why this instead of an editable grid</h2>
    <p>Bulk data maintenance splits into four patterns, and only one of them
    wants a grid. Row-swap inline edit covers config tables
    (<a href={base + '/components/inline-editing'}>inline editing</a>);
    master-detail covers records with more fields than fit a row;
    <em>mass change</em> covers "set field X for N rows"; and this pattern
    covers true bulk work, where the editing happens in the user's
    spreadsheet — which beats any web grid at being a spreadsheet — and the
    web's job is to validate and report. Reaching for a grid to avoid building this one is how a screen
    ends up with 200 hand-edits and no audit of what failed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 3 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/alerts'}>Alerts</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/badge'}>Badge</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/patterns/bulk-actions', 'Bulk actions'],
    ['/components/data-table', 'Data table'],
    ['/components/file-upload', 'File upload'],
    ['/patterns/validation-summary', 'Validation summary'],
    ['/patterns/wizard', 'Multi-step wizard (a guided single record, vs. this batch)'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
